<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Merneptah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 1213–1203 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century-bce">13th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Merneptah was the fourth king of the 19th Dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, son and successor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a>. In his fifth year he defeated a Libyan invasion, joined by contingents of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-peoples">Sea Peoples</a>, at Perire in the western Delta. His victory stele, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merneptah-stele">Merneptah Stele</a>, contains the earliest known mention of Israel.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/merneptah.jpg" alt="Merneptah" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hyspaosines · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ramses_and_the_Pharaohs%27_Gold,_London_2026-03-06_-_149_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Merneptah was probably the fourth child of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a> and Isetnofret and his thirteenth son. He came to power only because all his older brothers had died, and he was the first royal-born king since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tutankhamun">Tutankhamun</a>. He appears as a prince on family stelae from Aswan and Silsila, dated to the thirtieth and thirty-third or thirty-fourth years of Ramesses II, alongside his parents, his sister Bintanath and his brothers Ramesses and Khaemwaset.</p><p>Little else is known of him before the fortieth year of his father's reign, when he became Overseer of the Army. After the death of his brother Khaemwaset in the fifty-fifth year, he was proclaimed crown prince and served as prince regent for the last twelve years of the old king's life. Mummy studies suggest that he was over fifty when he took the throne. His Great Royal Wife was Isetnofret II, probably his full sister or his niece.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Merneptah moved the administrative centre from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pi-ramesses">Pi-Ramesses</a>, his father's capital, back to Memphis, where he built a palace beside the temple of Ptah. He ruled for almost ten years, from 13 August 1213 BCE until his death on 2 May 1203 BCE.</p><p>In his fifth year <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libyans">Libyans</a> threatened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> from the west, helped by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-peoples">Sea Peoples</a>, whom the texts name as the Ekwesh, Teresh, Lukka, Sherden and Shekelesh. The Libyan chief Meryre, son of Ded, had reached the fields of Perire, probably on the western edge of the Delta. Merneptah's account, inscribed beside the sixth pylon at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a> and on the Athribis Stele, tells of a victorious battle of six hours in which the invaders threw down their weapons and fled. He claims to have killed 6,000 soldiers and taken 9,000 prisoners.</p><p>The same events appear in a poem on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merneptah-stele">Merneptah Stele</a>, also called the Israel Stele. It also mentions the suppression of revolts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a> and says that 'Israel has been wiped out... its seed is no more', the first known ancient Egyptian record of Israel, named as a people rather than a country or city.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Merneptah died on 2 May 1203 BCE after a reign of almost ten years. He was buried in tomb KV8 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-the-kings">Valley of the Kings</a>, but his mummy was moved in ancient times and was found in 1898 among those in the tomb of Amenhotep II. It showed that he had suffered from arthritis and atherosclerosis and was about 1.71 metres tall. His son Seti II succeeded him, though not without challenge from a rival king, Amenmesse, who seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a> and Kush during Seti's reign.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Merneptah” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Bronze Age: the list of the pharaoh Seti I from c. 1215 BCE, the chronicle of a campaign by Ramesses III, and a letter from a scribe at a border fortress in the Wadi Tumilat under <b>Merneptah</b> reporting &quot;shasu-tribes of Edom&quot; moving to watering holes in Egyptian territory.</blockquote><a href="/empire/edom">Edom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The people of Israel appear in history in the late second millennium BCE: an Egyptian inscription of the pharaoh <b>Merneptah</b>, around 1208 BCE, names “Israel” as a people in Canaan.</blockquote><a href="/religion/judaism">Judaism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>At the end of the period, Egyptian inscriptions of <b>Merneptah</b> and Ramesses III list among the Sea Peoples groups such as the Ekwesh and the Denyen, whom some scholars connect with the Achaeans and the Danaans.</blockquote><a href="/culture/mycenaean-civilisation">Mycenaean civilisation · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1208 BCE their chief Meryey, son of Did, led a coalition including Sea Peoples against <b>Merneptah</b>, who defeated it at Perire and claimed over six thousand Libyans killed.</blockquote><a href="/state/libu">Libu</a></li><li><blockquote>Lukka appear among the Sea Peoples who attacked Egypt under <b>Merneptah</b> in the late 13th century BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/lukka">Lukka</a></li></ul></section>
